how does the decision-making process in  the Council of the European Union work  well the council is one of the three  main institutions of the EU with the  Commission and the Parliament it is  composed of national government  ministers from each of the 28 member  states ministers represent the interests  of their own democratically accountable  governments the council in the  parliament consider amend negotiate and  adopt EU legislation normally proposed  by the commission that is the ordinary  legislative procedure also known as  co-decision it sounds simple but it's  not that easy under the ordinary  legislative procedure the council and  the Parliament are on equal footing if  they reach agreement they can adopt the  legislation at first reading if not  there is a second reading if following  the second reading the two institutions  still can't agree a conciliation  committee is convened since 1999  sixty-seven percent of ordinary  legislative procedure files have been  concluded during the first reading  twenty-four percent during the second  reading and only nine percent through  conciliation  there are two other procedures used for  a few specific cases the consent  procedure and the consultation procedure  once adopted certain types of EU  legislation then have to be incorporated  international law some are directly  applicable others take some time to  become law for the 500 million citizens  of the EU how can ministers hold their  national jobs and sit in the council  well they get a little help all the work  of the council is prepared or  coordinated by the permanent  representatives committee known as Cora  / Cora / is made up of member state  representatives in addition the council  has more than 150 working parties and  committees who also help few those  ministers are human after all in short  that's how decision-making in the  Council of the EU works find out more on  the website at Consilium shrubbery you
 

